menu "WS2812B Clock Remote Logging"

    config LOGSHIP_ENABLE
        bool "Ship log lines over the network"
        default n
        help
            Copy every log line to a syslog collector over UDP, so the clock
            can be watched without a serial cable. Sending never blocks the
            task that logged: lines are queued and a sender task posts them.

    config LOGSHIP_HOST
        string "Collector hostname or IP"
        depends on LOGSHIP_ENABLE
        default ""
        help
            Where to send syslog datagrams. A name is resolved once and
            re-resolved if sending starts to fail.

    config LOGSHIP_PORT
        int "Collector UDP port"
        depends on LOGSHIP_ENABLE
        range 1 65535
        default 5514
        help
            514 is the well-known syslog port, but binding it needs root on
            the collector; 5514 is the usual unprivileged stand-in.

    config LOGSHIP_APP_NAME
        string "syslog APP-NAME field"
        depends on LOGSHIP_ENABLE
        default "ws2812bclock"

    config LOGSHIP_HOSTNAME
        string "syslog HOSTNAME field"
        depends on LOGSHIP_ENABLE
        default ""
        help
            Leave blank to use the device's own network hostname, which is
            what distinguishes one clock from another in the log stream.

    config LOGSHIP_LINE_MAX
        int "Longest log line shipped (bytes)"
        depends on LOGSHIP_ENABLE
        range 128 1024
        default 192
        help
            Longer lines are truncated. This is also the per-slot cost of the
            queue below, so the two together set how much RAM logging uses.

    config LOGSHIP_QUEUE_DEPTH
        int "Lines held while the link is down"
        depends on LOGSHIP_ENABLE
        range 4 128
        default 32
        help
            Lines queue up until the collector is reachable, so the boot
            messages survive the wait for WiFi. Once it is full the newest
            lines are dropped and a count of them is sent later.

    config LOGSHIP_REPORT_COREDUMP
        bool "Report a stored core dump on boot"
        depends on ESP_COREDUMP_ENABLE_TO_FLASH && ESP_COREDUMP_DATA_FORMAT_ELF
        default y
        help
            After a crash, log the faulting task, the program counter, the
            exception cause and a backtrace. That goes out over the network
            like any other log line, so a crash can be diagnosed without
            plugging the clock in. The dump itself stays in flash for
            "idf.py coredump-info".

endmenu
